
## Phase 3 Results (built 2026-09-02; tested in isolation, then applied and re-verified live)

**Implementation deviation, stated plainly**: keysync is implemented in **Node**, not the existing Rust crate (`C:\Users\osami\.uw\keysync\{keysync.mjs,run.mjs,verify-prune.mjs,test-all.mjs}`). All of Phase 3's acceptance criteria are behavioural and are enforced in code; a Rust port can follow. The Rust crate and toolchain are both present and untouched.

**Vault arithmetic confirmed exactly as the plan states**: 56 registry keys → **46 after the filter** → **44 distinct providers**. `google` collapses to a single key post-filter, so only `groq` and `deepseek` reach the tie-break, as the plan says.

**Multi-key tie-breaks resolved by name, not timestamp** (`KEY_CHOICES`): `groq` → `personal.groq.free` (the plan flagged that a 19-second gap would otherwise route personal traffic through the institutional `tamu` key — the personal key is chosen deliberately); `deepseek` → `personal_maestro.deepseek.paid`. keysync **throws** rather than guessing if any other multi-key provider appears.

**What actually works, measured rather than assumed.** Of 44 wired providers (83 picker rows), **21 rows across 19 providers return genuine completions**; after pruning to verified rows, **21/21 shipped rows work**. The rest fail upstream for reasons unrelated to routing: HTTP 402 payment-required (`cerebras`, `chutes`, `deepseek`, `pollinations`, `routllm`, `sambanova`, `veniceai`), 401/403 bad-or-expired keys (`agentrouter`, `indeedwebid`, `xai`, `aihubmix`), 503 provider-down (`gorouter`, `seekai`, `tabiai`, `tokenrouter`), plus assorted 400/404s and one `nvidia` timeout. Working set: agnes, aionlabs, alibaba, bai, bigmodel, cloudflare, cohere, fanar, gmicloudai, google, groq, kilo, mistral, nscale, ollama, opencode, openrouter, orcarouter, tokenharbor.

**Finding 5 — the bundled catalog's model ids are frequently not callable.** Preferring `dist/models.json` ids over the vault's `testModel` produced a **4/44** live pass rate, almost all upstream 404s. The vault's `testModel` is the probe-verified id for that specific key, so keysync now leads with it and appends catalog entries as extras: **4/44 → 17/44**, then 19 providers after probing alternates. This inverts the plan's assumption that the bundled catalog is the better source — it is richer, but far less trustworthy per key.

**Finding 6 — `behavesAs` is required, not optional.** Its accepted value is the id of a model this Claude Code version knows (e.g. `claude-opus-4-8`) whose client-side handling to reuse. Without it every launch warns `unrecognized_model` and assumes a 200k context window. With `behavesAs` set on every row, the warnings disappear entirely.

**Finding 7 — the profile anchor must be capability-chosen, not fastest.** Picking the lowest-latency verified model (`groq/allam-2-7b`) passed a bare probe but returned `API Error: 400 All target providers failed` under Claude Code's real payload (full system prompt + tool definitions). Anchoring to `google/gemini-3.5-flash-lite` fixed it. Small models pass synthetic probes and fail real traffic.

**Finding 8 — setting only `profile.model` leaves background traffic broken**, exactly as round 3 predicted. CCR derived `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL` itself (`orcarouter/auto`, not even a picker row) and title generation warned on every run. keysync now sets `model`, `smallFastModel`, `haikuModel`, `sonnetModel`, `opusModel` and `fableModel` together, and the warning is gone.

**The `[1m]` defect is resolved.** keysync owns the final settings write, so after CCR's `applyProfile` it strips the `[1m]` suffix from every model env var (7 per run). Verified zero remain.

**End-to-end, through Claude Code, no warnings**: a normal prompt returns `E2E-OK`, and `--model mistral/mistral-small-latest` returns `SWITCH-OK` — a genuine cross-provider switch. Both ran with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` and a scrubbed env, against the isolated gateway, with live state verified unchanged.

**Still not exercised**: the interactive `/model` picker (needs a TTY), and the live cutover (blocked — see below).

**Finding 9 — the design as specified REMOVES Claude itself, and that is a product defect, not an implementation bug.** The live cutover was performed 2026-09-01 21:08 and had to be rolled back immediately. It worked exactly as this document specifies, and that is the problem: the vault filter excludes `anthropic` as an orphan, so CCR has **zero Anthropic providers**; `applyProfile` repoints every model-alias env var (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `_OPUS_`, `_SONNET_`, `_HAIKU_`, `_FABLE_`) at a third-party model; and `replaceBuiltInOptions: true` strips the built-in rows from `/model`. Net effect: Claude Code stops being able to run Claude at all — Opus/Sonnet become unreachable and the picker offers only third-party models. Phase 3's `replaceBuiltInOptions` bullet reasoned that built-in rows would be "decoys that appear to work but 401/misroute"; that reasoning is sound *given no Anthropic provider*, but the conclusion drawn from it was wrong. The right fix is to stop the premise being true — configure an Anthropic provider — not to hide the rows. **For a machine where Claude Code on Opus is the daily driver, the v2 design as written is a downgrade, and this was never surfaced as a question in any of the review rounds.**

**Required before any future cutover**: add an Anthropic provider to CCR so `/model` lists real Claude models alongside the third-party ones, making the cutover additive instead of destructive. The mechanism already exists on this machine and is documented in the user's own notes: a local OAuth relay at `~/.local/bin/anthropic-oauth-relay.mjs` (port 4517) that reads `~/.claude/.credentials.json` and is wired as an `anthropic_messages` provider with a dummy key and `baseUrl http://127.0.0.1:4517`. It must be running (no autostart) and its quota state verified — it was 429/quota-exhausted when last checked on 2026-08-31.

**Rollback note for whoever writes the next recovery script**: the ad-hoc PowerShell rollback used on 2026-09-01 introduced two defects of its own — `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8` wrote a **BOM**, leaving `settings.json` unparseable as JSON, and its env-key regex `^(CCR_|ANTHROPIC_|CODEXL_|CLAUDE_AGENT_API_BASE_URL|CLAUDE_CODE_)` also deleted the user's own `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS`, while removing the `model` pin. Prefer restoring from keysync's own `settings.json.uw-backup-<timestamp>`, which is written immediately before every live write.

**Finding 9 RESOLVED (2026-09-02, isolated instance).** The Anthropic OAuth relay is now wired into keysync as a first-class provider (`ANTHROPIC_RELAY` / `ANTHROPIC_TIERS` in `keysync.mjs`), and the cutover is additive rather than destructive. Verified live through CCR's gateway: `claude-opus-5`, `claude-sonnet-5`, `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` and `claude-fable-5-1` all return real completions via the relay, with no Anthropic credential stored in CCR's config (the relay resolves the subscription token itself from `~/.claude/.credentials.json`). keysync now:
- probes the relay's `/health` first and **warns loudly** if it is down, because that is precisely the state that would silently remove Claude from Claude Code again (`--no-anthropic` opts out deliberately);
- sets **per-tier** anchors instead of pointing every tier at one model — `model`/`opusModel` → `claude-opus-5`, `sonnetModel` → `claude-sonnet-5`, `haikuModel`/`smallFastModel` → `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`, `fableModel` → `claude-fable-5-1`, so background work stays on the cheap tier;
- emits Claude rows **without** `behavesAs` (Claude Code already knows those ids) and third-party rows with it.

Result in the isolated instance: **20 providers, 25 picker rows**, Claude first. End-to-end through Claude Code, all passing and warning-free: default (`claude-opus-5`) → `CLAUDE-DEFAULT-OK`; `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5` → `SONNET-OK`; `google/gemini-3.5-flash-lite` → `GEMINI-OK`; `mistral/mistral-small-latest` → `MISTRAL-OK`; `anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` → `HAIKU-OK`. Switching across Claude ↔ third-party works in both directions.

**Operational precondition for the relay**: it has no autostart and must be running before any cutover or `/model` selection of a Claude row (`node ~/.local/bin/anthropic-oauth-relay.mjs`, port 4517). If it dies, every Claude row stops serving while third-party rows keep working. Its quota is the user's real Claude subscription.

**Live cutover status: DONE 2026-09-02, and it holds.** With the relay running, `run.mjs --target live --verified-only --i-know` applied cleanly: CCR listening on 3456/3457/3458, `apiKeyHelper` + `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` + a 22-row `modelPicker` all present in the real `~/.claude/settings.json`, all 19 pre-existing top-level keys preserved (including the user's own `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS`), and `profile.model` = `anthropic/claude-opus-5`. Verified with real `claude -p` runs: the default answers as Claude Opus 5 through the relay, and `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5`, `anthropic/claude-fable-5-1`, `google/gemini-3.5-flash-lite` and `cloudflare/@cf/openai/gpt-oss-120b` all return correct content. Claude is preserved and third-party models are additive — the Finding 9 failure mode is closed.

**Finding 10 — synthetic gateway probes systematically over-report what works, and only the real client is trustworthy.** `groq/openai/gpt-oss-20b` returns HTTP 200 to a hand-built gateway request yet fails with `400 All target providers failed` under Claude Code. The difference was chased down methodically and is *not*: streaming (both stream and non-stream return 200), the `?beta=true` query param Claude Code appends (both return 200), a system prompt, a single tool definition, or provider headers (44 of 47 vault providers use plain `Authorization: Bearer`, so `headersTemplate` is a non-issue). CCR's own request log confirms the failing call resolved correctly (`requestedModel`/`resolvedModel` both `groq/openai/gpt-oss-20b`, `client: Profile: Claude Code`) and failed at the upstream with a 400. What remains is payload scale — Claude Code sends roughly fifteen tool schemas plus a long system prompt, which small/reasoning models reject. **Consequence for the plan: any acceptance criterion phrased as "a request through the gateway succeeds" is too weak.** The gate must be the real client. `verify-cli.mjs` implements this — it runs `claude -p --model <row>` for every picker row, sequentially (parallel launches produce spurious rate-limit failures), and prunes to rows that genuinely work. This supersedes the synthetic probe in `verify-prune.mjs`, which is retained only as a fast pre-filter.

**Also confirmed**: the `ai-gateway` prefix-strip patch recorded in the user's notes is **gone**, wiped by Phase 0.5's `npm i -g` reinstall exactly as that phase's follow-up predicted — the backup `dist/index.js.bak-prefixfix` no longer exists either. groq's two-slash model id (`groq/openai/gpt-oss-20b`) nonetheless resolves and serves correctly at the gateway, so the patch appears unnecessary in 3.0.22.

**Phase 3 acceptance criteria — closed out 2026-09-02.** Implemented in `safety.mjs` and wired into the write path:
- **Stale WIF token deletion — DONE.** `ccr-claude-code-wif-token-<profile>.txt` is deleted after every `applyProfile`, pinned to the exact profile filename (a glob would destroy other profiles' tokens) and recurring by design, since any CCR interaction recreates it. Verified: only the `apiKeyHelper` `.cmd` remains, so the plaintext gateway-key copies drop from two to one.
- **`config.sqlite` backup — DONE, WAL-safe.** `VACUUM INTO` (never a file copy, which would omit `-wal`/`-shm` and restore to a silently stale state), then DPAPI-encrypted at rest and the plaintext intermediate removed. Lives in a fixed `%LOCALAPPDATA%\uw-keysync\backups` with an owner-only DACL.
- **Rollback — DONE.** The merge/write/verify steps run inside a `try/catch`; on failure `settings.json` is restored from its pre-write backup, both restore points are kept, a decrypt command for the DB snapshot is printed, and the run exits non-zero. Implemented and reviewed but **not yet exercised by a real failure**.
- **Retention — DONE.** The DB snapshot carries every provider key, so it is deleted as soon as the write verifies; the `settings.json` backup carries no provider keys and is the user's rollback path, so exactly the newest is kept and older ones pruned (5 stale files cleaned on first run). Failed-run snapshots are capped at 2.
- **Credential hygiene — verified by inspection**, not merely asserted: `verified-rows.json` and `last-test-results.json` contain zero credential-shaped matches, and keys are never logged (only provider ids and status codes).
- **`cargo-audit` — moot** under the Node deviation; keysync has zero third-party dependencies (Node builtins and `fetch` only).
- **Provider-count criterion — deliberately superseded.** The plan required `Providers[]` to be exactly 44. keysync builds all 44, then ships only rows proven to work (`--verified-only`): currently **14 providers / 18 rows**, all verified through the real Claude Code client. Shipping 44 would mean shipping rows that 402/401/503 when selected. The count criterion should be read as "all 44 are built and considered", not "44 are shipped".

**Final verified state (live)**: 18 picker rows across 14 providers, all confirmed working through real `claude -p` runs — the four Anthropic models plus alibaba, agnes, bai, bigmodel, cloudflare, gmicloudai, google, kilo, nscale, ollama, opencode, openrouter, tokenharbor. Four rows were pruned for cause: `groq/openai/gpt-oss-20b` and `cohere/command-r7b-12-2024` (400 under Claude Code's full tool payload — the Finding 10 class), `fanar/Fanar` (Claude Code rejects its response as too large), and `aionlabs/aion-3.0-mini` (exceeded a 120s timeout).

**Known live-config tradeoff, surfaced not absorbed**: with `apiKeyHelper` set, every Claude Code launch prints `claude.ai connectors are disabled because ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or another auth source is set and takes precedence over your claude.ai login`. Org/claude.ai connectors are unavailable while UW is active. Reverting is a single restore of `settings.json.uw-backup-<timestamp>`. Starting the live CCR daemon was refused by the permission classifier, and the `settings.json` write inherently changes how running Claude Code sessions route — the precise mechanism of the 2026-09-01 outage. Both need explicit user action. `run.mjs` supports `--target live --i-know` (full) and `--no-profile` (gateway only, writes no settings file).

## Phase 4 — Runtime refresh wiring

- **Manual on-demand refresh** (slash command or standalone script invocation) is the only trigger in v2's initial scope, per "Cut from v2" above.
- Any future trigger (the deferred `SessionStart` hook, Phase 6) must be built on: content-diff-and-skip against the live config (not a time throttle — CCR's restart trigger is a content diff per the established fact above, so comparing before writing eliminates gratuitous restarts *and* reduces staleness, for free, once the deterministic-serialization requirement from Phase 3 is met); a lockfile so two concurrent Claude Code sessions don't both trigger keysync at once (unaddressed in the original draft — two simultaneous `saveConfig` calls is a lost-update race on top of a double restart); and a gateway-health poll before returning control, so the *first* prompt after a triggered restart doesn't land mid-restart.
- Manual refresh's acceptance criteria: running it produces the same acceptance-criteria outcomes as Phase 3's; a deliberately-broken run (e.g. bad vault path) logs an error clearly and does not leave CCR or `settings.json` in a half-written state (per Phase 3's rollback requirement).
- ~~**Open question**: does Claude Code re-read `modelPicker` mid-session?~~ **ANSWERED 2026-09-02 — NO.** Tested exactly as this bullet specified: a distinctively-labelled row was appended to the live `settings.json` at 01:36 while two Claude Code sessions (started 00:21 and 00:23) were running; invoking `/model` in one of them did not show it. Settings are read at process start. **Consequence: the `SessionStart` hook is DROPPED, not deferred.** Its entire purpose was to refresh the catalog for the session being started, and it cannot — a hook fires after settings are already parsed, so any regen it triggers is invisible until the *next* launch. Two of the original four objections to the hook had since become void (gratuitous restarts, now eliminated by content-diff-and-skip; and the watchdog restart-loop, now void with the watchdog deprecated), so this test was the deciding one — and it decided against. Phase 6 item 2's freshness wording is correspondingly "one session behind", not "at most one session-start old".

## Phase 5 — End-to-end resilience test (release gate)

Full loop: keysync runs (restart simply accepted — with the watchdog deprecated there is no healer to suppress) → `/model` shows the fresh, correctly-labeled catalog → select a model → live prompt produces a genuine, correctly-routed response → **mid-session, same conversation**, use `/model` again to switch to a second, structurally different provider → confirm the next turn is genuinely served by the new model (not silently falling back to the first, per the `Router.fallback` risk above) and completes without error → kill the gateway mid-session → confirm the supervisor restarts it (Phase 1's replacement; note its check interval is ~5 min, so this test must allow for that latency rather than expecting instant recovery) → confirm `/model` and routing still work post-recovery → confirm a `modelPicker` entry pointing at a since-removed provider fails cleanly. That last check needs a **remediation branch, not just a hoped-for outcome**: given `ModelRegistry.resolve()` returning undefined causes CCR to proceed into whatever fallback chain is configured rather than error (established fact above), this test is only meaningful once Phase 3's `Router.fallback` assertion is in place (empty chain, or a chain Phase 5 explicitly accounts for). **The stale-entry test case must be a bare, unqualified model id (e.g. `deepseek-chat`, not a namespaced string like `openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-chat`)** — a namespaced id is close to guaranteed to fail cleanly (no provider will have that literal compound string in its `models[]`), which makes it the *safest* possible test case and proves nothing about the actual risk. The real danger is a bare id that happens to exactly match (case-insensitively) exactly one of the 44 configured providers' model names, which resolves via the cross-provider fallback stage in the established fact above — silently routing to a provider the picker label never named. Confirm the test asserts *which provider actually served the response*, not merely that a response came back.

**Cross-model continuity, reframed from v1 and further narrowed this round**: v1's biggest untested risk was whether `--continue`/`--resume` correctly reloads a saved session transcript across a relaunch into a different provider. v2's live, same-session `/model` switch removes the relaunch/session-file-reload version of that risk — there's no relaunch, Claude Code already holds the conversation in memory. The risk is **narrowed and relocated, not eliminated**: (a) mid-session — whether prior-turn history shaped for one backend (e.g. Anthropic-native tool-call or extended-thinking content blocks) causes an error when the next turn routes through CCR's wire-format translation to a structurally different backend; (b) at resume time — if a session whose last turns were served by provider B is later resumed with `--continue`/`--resume`, Claude Code reloads that B-shaped transcript while `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` (from `profile.model`, per Phase 3) may point at provider A — the same hazard as v1's, just relocated to resume instead of switch. Test both explicitly, plus two more concrete, more-likely-than-translation-errors failure modes named by review rather than left implicit: (c) **context-window mismatch** — switching mid-session to a smaller-context-window model with a large accumulated transcript should fail clearly on the next turn, not truncate silently; deliberately include a small-context second provider in this test rather than two similarly-sized ones; (d) **mid-tool-call switching** — an assistant `tool_use` block whose `tool_result` arrives after the switch.

**Additional acceptance criteria this round**: the picker is actually checked for usability at the real 44-provider scale, not left unassessed (Phase 6 item 1 names this the most likely regression and, without this check, nothing ever verifies whether it's actually a problem in practice) — record how many rows render, whether `/model`'s list is at all navigable at that count, and file the result as the first real observation gating Phase 6 item 1's curation decision.

Non-goals (carried forward from v1, updated): free mode, cost/latency auto-routing, credential rotation/expiry, cross-machine UW config sync, profile allowlists/per-key rate limits/context-archive (excluded by the single-key design), a fuzzy-search/nested picker UI beyond native `/model` (Phase 6), CCR's system-proxy transport (permanently rejected, not deferred), the `SessionStart` hook (cut from v2, see above).

## Phase 6 — Future Improvements (backlog, explicitly deferred — not in v2's initial scope)

v2 accepts three known tradeoffs relative to v1's live-searchable TUI, deliberately, with eyes open. None of the three are solved by v2's initial ship. Each is listed here as **DEFERRED** with its current status, its concrete future fix, and — new this round — an explicit gate/trigger so "revisit later" has an actual observation attached to it rather than staying open indefinitely.

**1. Picker UI richness/search — DEFERRED, no mitigation shipped in v2.** `modelPicker` is a flat list with no confirmed search/filter, and 44 providers × several models each could make it a long plain scroll — the most likely real UX regression vs. v1, and the one item in this section with a real risk of shipping unassessed if nobody checks it. **Gate: Phase 5's picker-usability observation (added this round) is the trigger** — if that check finds the list genuinely hard to navigate, this item is promoted out of backlog; if not, it stays deferred.
- *Curate, don't dump*: have keysync emit only 1–3 flagship models per provider by default instead of the full catalog, cutting entry count significantly; make the cutoff configurable. (Must stay in sync with `Providers[].models` per Phase 3's cross-product requirement — curating the picker without curating the provider's configured models breaks routing.)
- *Test native type-to-jump*: check whether Claude Code's `/model` list jumps to the first match as you type, even without full fuzzy filtering — undocumented, untested, possibly a free partial win.
- *Optional standalone fuzzy-search tool*: revive `menu.rs`/`ui.rs` (kept in git history from Phase 3, not deleted) as an opt-in secondary tool for when the native list is too long — still targeting the same `modelPicker` JSON or calling `--model` directly, so it stays additive rather than replacing the native path.
- *Track upstream*: if Claude Code ships native `/model` search later, this item may become unnecessary.

**2. Catalog freshness — DEFERRED for a full fix; the manual-refresh path in Phase 4 is the only mitigation shipped, and it requires you to remember to run it.** v1's live TUI queried providers at browse-time, always current. v2's `modelPicker` is pre-baked by keysync — **and, corrected on review, "run keysync more often" does not actually buy freshness the way earlier drafts implied**: the catalog source is `dist/models.json`, a static file CCR itself ships with a fixed `generatedAt` timestamp. Re-running keysync re-reads the same frozen snapshot; the real freshness ceiling is CCR's own release cadence, not keysync's trigger frequency. What keysync's manual refresh *does* still buy: picking up a vault key you just added or changed (a real, useful trigger), just not a newer model catalog. **Gate, restated so it's an actual promotion trigger rather than a question that can be answered either way**: this item is promoted out of backlog only if the manual-refresh path proves to be real day-to-day friction in practice. Building the `SessionStart` hook additionally requires both Phase 4's open question about `/model`'s re-read behavior being answered *favorably* (not just answered) — the restart-suppression precondition is **void**, since the watchdog that required it was deprecated (Phase 1). Don't build it speculatively on the remaining partial precondition.
- ~~Future: the `SessionStart` hook~~ — **DROPPED 2026-09-02**, not deferred: its precondition was tested and failed (Claude Code does not re-read `modelPicker` mid-session, so a hook cannot help the session it fires in). Do not revive it without new evidence that this behaviour changed.
- Further out: push-based invalidation (a vault-change watcher triggering immediate regen) instead of relying on session starts or manual triggers.

**3. Ad-hoc flexibility — DEFERRED, and reframed this round: the mitigation is not "check if `/model` accepts free text," it's "keep `Providers[].models` broad enough."** v1's live TUI let you browse/type freely. The original draft of this item hoped that if `/model` still accepts typed input alongside a curated `modelPicker`, the tradeoff "mostly evaporates on its own." **That's refuted by the established fact above**: `ModelRegistry.resolve()` matches (case-insensitively) against the provider's *configured* `models[]` regardless of what Claude Code's own UI accepts — so a freely typed model keysync hasn't written into `Providers[]` will not resolve no matter what the picker's input behavior turns out to be. **Corrected further this round**: Phase 4's manual refresh only helps for a model that's simply missing because a vault key hasn't been synced yet — given `dist/models.json` is a static file (Phase 6 item 2, above), a manual refresh cannot pull in a model that isn't in that file at all, for any of the 23-of-44 providers currently falling back to a single `testModel` entry (Phase 0.5's coverage-gap note). For those providers, "ad-hoc flexibility" genuinely has no mitigation in v2's scope beyond hand-editing `Providers[].models` directly. **Gate: none needed — the free-text question is settled, not open, and dropped; the coverage-gap limitation is tracked as a known, permanent-for-now constraint, not something a future refresh trigger will fix.**

**4. Multi-model coverage per provider — DEFERRED to a later discussion (added 2026-09-02, user-requested).** Today the live picker shows **1 model for 12 of the 14 shipped providers** (only `anthropic` with 4 and `alibaba` with 2 have more). keysync actually *builds* **87 rows across 45 providers**, so this is not the curation cap doing it — the cap is 3 (`UW_MAX_MODELS`). Three separate causes, only one of which is a deliberate choice:

- **(a) A real defect — the verification ratchet.** `verify-cli.mjs` probes only the rows in the *current* `modelPicker`, not the full built set, so once a row is pruned it can never return. `mistral/mistral-small-latest` was dropped for a **transient 503** (it had passed minutes earlier) and cannot come back on any subsequent run. This is a bug, not a tradeoff, and it is cheap to fix independently of the rest of this item: probe the built set, not the shipped set.
- **(b) Data gap.** 23 of 44 providers have no entry in CCR's bundled `dist/models.json` at all, so exactly one model id is known for them (the vault's `testModel`). No amount of curation policy changes that.
- **(c) Catalog unreliability.** For covered providers, bundled catalog ids frequently are not callable with the actual key/tier (Finding 5).

**The real fix is live per-provider `/v1/models` discovery** — asking each provider what *this key* can actually call, rather than trusting a shared static file. The vault's own `providers.json` notes exactly this for many entries ("GET /v1/models for full current catalog").

**This reopens a decision the plan already made.** "Cut from v2" item 2 removed live per-provider fetch on the reasoning that the catalog source is a static file, making a cache hierarchy pointless layering. **Measurement has since invalidated that premise**: the static file covers only half the providers and its ids often do not work. The cut was correct given what was believed then and is wrong given what is now known — live discovery is now evidence-backed rather than speculative, and if it ships, `http.rs`-equivalent safety constraints already specified in Phase 3 apply (https-only, `redirect::Policy::none()`, no credential replay to a redirect host).

**Gate**: user has deferred this for a later discussion. When it is taken up, the open sub-questions are the per-provider cap (a flat picker at 14 providers × N models becomes the Phase 6 item 1 usability problem — the picker is confirmed usable at 18 rows, unmeasured beyond that), and whether discovery results are cached and for how long.

## Risks and Mitigations (v2)

- `modelPicker` schema or `--model`-string acceptance changes in a future Claude Code version → re-verify against the live settings-schema validation error (not public docs, which don't cover this) before any keysync release.
- CCR changes `resolveClaudeCodeGatewayAuthMode`'s version gate or default, or removes the "legacy" `api-key-helper` path entirely, in a future release → `CCR_CLAUDE_CODE_AUTH_MODE=api-key-helper` is an explicit override and should remain stable even if the *default* changes, but CCR's own source treats it as legacy — re-verify after any CCR upgrade, don't assume permanence.
- CCR's routing state (`Router.rules`, `Router.fallback`, `profile.availableModels`) diverges from what keysync last wrote/assumed → Phase 3's explicit assertions (fail loudly, don't proceed) rather than assuming the clean-install defaults hold.
- keysync's `Providers[]`/`modelPicker` writes race CCR's own background auto-refresh cycle → `autoFetchModels: false` on owned entries (Phase 3) plus minimizing the read→mutate→write window.
- The two writers within one keysync run (CCR's own `saveConfig` read-modify-write, and keysync's direct `modelPicker` merge) race each other → explicit ordering sequence, re-read-and-verify step (Phase 3).
- Long flat `modelPicker` list is a real UX regression vs. v1's searchable TUI → explicitly accepted, tracked as Phase 6 item 1 with a real usability check as its gate (Phase 5), not silently absorbed.
- A stale `modelPicker` entry (provider removed from vault since last keysync) selected by the user → CCR's own fallback-chain behavior on an unresolved model can silently misroute rather than error; must be tested against the *real* `Router.fallback` state, not the clean-install default (Phase 5).
- Cross-model mid-session switch sends backend-A-shaped history to backend B, at switch time and separately at resume time → both tested explicitly (Phase 5), not assumed safe.
- Context-window mismatch or a mid-flight tool-call on a cross-model switch → both named and tested explicitly (Phase 5), not left implicit under a general "wire-format translation" umbrella that wouldn't have caught them.
- Proxy-mode transport tempting as a "just enable it" fallback if `api-key-helper` ever regresses → explicitly and permanently rejected (CA-cert trust-store install), documented here so it isn't quietly reconsidered later without the same scrutiny.
- Credential storage moves from DPAPI-encrypted to CCR's plaintext `config.sqlite`, additionally exposed via the `getConfig` RPC → explicit accepted-tradeoff writeup (top of document), scoped by keeping `Providers[]` to only the filtered/needed set, and by Phase 3's logging-redaction and backup-lifecycle requirements.
- CCR writes two plaintext copies of the gateway key (WIF file + apiKeyHelper file) regardless of auth mode → Phase 3 deletes the stale one keysync's own mode change makes irrelevant.
- Provider fetch code (if the narrow `http.rs` live-probe path is kept, see Phase 3) following a redirect could replay a credential to an unintended host → `redirect::Policy::none()` + https-only enforcement, structural not just filter-based (Phase 3).
- A CI/build/watcher process accidentally commits keysync's cache/state files → explicit pinned locations + `.gitignore` coverage (Phase 3).
- ~~The watchdog restarts a gateway keysync itself is intentionally restarting~~ → **risk eliminated, not mitigated**: the healing watchdog was deprecated (Phase 1), so nothing exists that could fight a keysync restart. The replacement supervisor only ever starts a process that is not listening.
- The CCR gateway or the OAuth relay dies and stays dead, taking all of Claude Code with it (both are now in the critical path, which they were not before UW) → OS-level supervision restarts either within ~5 minutes (Phase 1). Residual: a process that starts and then fails *while listening* would not be caught — supervision checks liveness, not health.
- Two Claude Code sessions trigger keysync concurrently → lockfile (Phase 4).
- Background/small-fast-tier Claude Code traffic silently breaks because keysync only sets `profile.model`, not `profile.smallFastModel`/`opusModel`/etc., which CCR clears on every run regardless → Phase 3 sets all of them, Phase 5 tests background traffic explicitly, not just the main conversation.
- Two fresh "verified live" overclaims were found and corrected in this round's re-review (the `modelPicker`/routing half of the design is schema-derived, not live-tested, until Phase 2.5 passes) → language throughout this document now scopes claims to exactly what was tested, specifically to avoid Phase 2.5 being skipped on the strength of an overclaim.
- CCR appends `[1m]` to `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` for 1M-capable models and Claude Code rejects the suffixed id, breaking small/fast background traffic → observed live in Phase 2.5; must be resolved before Phase 3, since keysync sets these env vars on every run.
- CCR overrides a provider's explicitly configured protocol with a host/provider-inferred one → observed live in Phase 2.5 (`openai_chat_completions` + `protocolDetectionMode: "manual"` both ignored); Phase 3 cannot assume the vault's `protocol` field is what CCR will use, and must verify per provider.
- An unrecognized provider-format model id is assumed to have a 200k context window unless a `behavesAs`/`modelOverrides` mapping or `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS` is supplied → affects the majority of the catalog (the 23 uncovered providers); adopt `behavesAs` in Phase 3's picker rows.
- The shipped CCR dist diverges from the TypeScript source this plan cites → re-verify load-bearing facts against `dist/main/cli.js`, not the source reference, before relying on them.
- 23 of 44 filtered vault providers have no entry in the bundled pricing catalog and fall to a single-model, untiered fallback → stated explicitly (Phase 0.5), Phase 3's sample criteria required to span both populations, not just the covered half.

## ADR (v2)

**Decision**: UW's role changes from a standalone live-rendered TUI that prints a launch command, to a regenerator ("keysync") that writes CCR's `Providers[]`, Claude Code's native `modelPicker`, and a one-time auth-mode fix — making model switching a live, native, same-session `/model` action with no relaunch and no custom UI in the critical path.

**Drivers**: (1) the user's explicit north-star requirement — "seamless... at the same time robust, reliable, efficient, optimized, and support ALL my provider's API keys" — is better served by a zero-relaunch, zero-copy-paste design than by a live-queried standalone TUI; (2) the belief that full native `/model` nesting was "not architecturally possible" turned out to be wrong once tested directly; (3) v1's real blocker was never architectural — it was one CCR default (`wif` mode) with a one-line documented override, plus (found only after this round's review) a runtime-environment gap between how that fix was first verified and how v2 actually ships, since closed by re-testing under the real conditions (Phase 2(d)).

**Alternatives considered**: (a) v1's printed-command design — the `Providers[]`-writing half of its foundation is shared with v2 and recoverable from git history if `modelPicker`'s behavior ever regresses, but the menu/UI rendering half (`menu.rs`/`ui.rs`) was already restructured away by Phase 3, so calling it a "valid fallback" overstates how quickly it could be revived — it's recoverable, not standing. (b) CCR's system-proxy transport for auth, riding on real OAuth login — technically viable but requires installing a MITM root CA into the Windows trust store; rejected as a standing security tradeoff, not reconsidered. (c) Keep v1's live TUI as the primary interface and layer `modelPicker` underneath it anyway — rejected as needless duplication. (d) **Use CCR's own routing surface directly — `Router.rules` or `virtualModelProfiles` — as the switching mechanism, writing no `modelPicker` at all** (missing from the original draft of this ADR, added on review): both are real, confirmed-live CCR features (`gateway/runtime-change.ts:27`, `routing/config-compiler.ts:120-169`) that could in principle drive model selection without touching Claude Code's settings.json. Rejected because neither produces a native `/model` UX — the whole point of this redesign — and `virtualModelProfiles` in particular is still an open, unscheduled question for whether it should appear as a pseudo-provider in keysync's own catalog-building logic (see Follow-ups).

**Why chosen (settled 2026-09-02 — both load-bearing assumptions now verified)**: `api-key-helper` auth is live-verified, including with real OAuth present under the actual no-wrapper runtime (Phase 2(d)). Provider-format ids route correctly — verified end to end through the real client against 14 providers, with attribution. And the `/model` picker itself, the last unknown and the one no synthetic test could reach, was confirmed working by the user against the live config. **The redesign's central bet — that a native `/model` experience was achievable without any custom UI — is now vindicated on evidence rather than on documentation.** Worth recording plainly, since this document spent three review rounds unable to settle it and one round wrongly assuming it: the belief that this was "not architecturally possible against closed-source Claude Code" was simply wrong, and testing it directly was what proved it.

**Consequences**: model switching has no relaunch, and the specific relaunch/session-file-reload version of the `--continue`-across-providers risk is gone, not deferred — but the risk is narrowed and relocated rather than eliminated outright: a same-session cross-backend switch and a later cross-backend *resume* both carry a version of it, plus two more concretely-named failure modes (context-window mismatch, mid-tool-call switching) this round's review surfaced that a vaguer "wire-format translation" framing would not have caught — all four are now explicit Phase 5 tests. The picker itself is less rich than v1's live-searchable TUI for large catalogs — accepted knowingly, tracked as Phase 6 item 1, gated on an actual usability observation rather than left to be silently discovered in practice. `modelPicker`'s behavior is grounded in Claude Code's own settings-schema validator, which is more authoritative than the sparse public docs but is still one CLI version's behavior — re-verify after any Claude Code upgrade the same way this session did. Separately, and new to this revision: keysync's write path introduces a real, explicitly-documented credential-storage tradeoff (DPAPI → CCR's plaintext store) and two settings.json writers whose ordering must be respected — both now have explicit mitigations in Phase 3, not just acknowledgment.

**Follow-ups (reordered 2026-09-02 by what is actually outstanding)**: (0) ~~the interactive `/model` picker~~ — **confirmed working**; (0b) ~~Phase 1's watchdog~~ — **deprecated and replaced by OS-level supervision**; (0c) **Phase 4's self-clobber hazard** is now the highest-value open item: refresh is specified to run from inside a Claude Code session, which would rewrite that session's own settings and restart the gateway beneath it — the exact 2026-09-01 outage mechanism, today guarded only by a flag; (1) ~~Phase 2.5's real-key spike~~ — done; (2) decide Phase 6's scope (curation cutoff, whether to revive the standalone fuzzy-search tool, whether to build the `SessionStart` hook) once each item's stated gate actually fires, not preemptively; (3) re-verify `CCR_CLAUDE_CODE_AUTH_MODE`'s continued effectiveness after any CCR version upgrade, given CCR's own source treats it as a legacy path; (4) whether `virtualModelProfiles`-synthesized model IDs should appear as a pseudo-provider in keysync's catalog-building logic — carried over from v1, still open and unscheduled, now also relevant to the rejected ADR alternative (d) above; (5) new this round: convert the two hand-made CCR patches (Phase 0.5 follow-up) into checked-in, reapply-verified patch files before they're silently lost to a reinstall.

## Changelog

- v1, Rounds 1→5 (RALPLAN-DR consensus): see original entries below.
  - Round 1→2: fixed false "CCR is installed" premise; found settings.json-direct write is refuted.
  - Round 2→3: fixed phantom "Option E"; found saveConfig's restart semantics don't work as assumed; found tier-label coverage gap and fixed fallback ordering.
  - Round 3→4: architecture pivot (launcher instead of config-writer), verified viable against real gateway auth code.
  - Round 4→5: corrected the 1M-context/User-Agent claim, the `config.APIKEY` safety assumption, the model env-var set (added Fable, dropped SmallFastModel), the `provider/model` mechanism explanation; added WIF constants, 503-disabled watchdog case, stale-printed-command check, and clarified Phase 3's parallel-shippable scope.
- v1 → v2 (single follow-up session): diagnosed the real "Not logged in" root cause (CCR's version-gated WIF auto-selection) and fixed it with a one-line profile env var; discovered `modelPicker`'s real schema and verbatim provider-format-id acceptance; investigated and permanently rejected CCR's system-proxy transport; redesigned the entire model-switching architecture around native `/model` + a keysync regenerator, eliminating the printed-command/relaunch design; reclassified roughly half of Phase 3's already-built UW crate code as no-longer-needed for the core flow, kept as Phase 6 backlog material rather than deleted; added a Future Improvements backlog for three known tradeoffs.
- v2 → v2, round 2 (same session, after routing through the standing pipeline's architect + critic + security-reviewer in parallel): critic returned REVISE with one CRITICAL finding (the auth-fix verification ran through CCR's wrapper, which scrubs env vars v2's actual wrapper-less runtime doesn't — closed this round via a live re-test under the real runtime, with real credentials present throughout, per Phase 2(d)); architect found the design accounted for `Providers[]` but not CCR's `Router.rules`/`fallback`/`availableModels`, nor who owns the session's default model — both folded into Phase 3 as explicit assertions and a required `profile.model` write; security-reviewer found an undocumented credential-storage tradeoff, an unconditionally-duplicated plaintext gateway-key file, missing log-redaction and backup-lifecycle requirements — all folded in as explicit Phase 3 requirements and a top-of-document accepted-tradeoff note; added Phase 2.5 (real-key spike, sequenced before the full build); cut the `SessionStart` hook and the four-stage discovery cache from v2's initial scope as premature complexity; renumbered the backlog phase from 7 to 6 to close a numbering gap; corrected several overstated or ambiguous claims.
- v2, round 9 (2026-09-02, code review of Phase 4 — REQUEST CHANGES, all blockers fixed): the reviewer decompiled CCR's **actual** restart predicate from the shipped dist instead of trusting the plan, and it reframed the whole feature. CCR restarts on `configChanged || _E(prev,next)`, where `_E` compares `gateway.*`, `observability.*` and `proxy.*` scalars plus `JSON.stringify` of `Providers`, **top-level `agent`**, `virtualModelProfiles`, `mediaTools`, `plugins`, `providerPlugins`, `proxy.targets/upstream` — and **not** `profile.*` or `Router.*`. My fingerprint was wrong in both directions: it compared `profile.profiles` (never a trigger, manufacturing false "changed") while omitting `observability.requestLogs`, **which `run.mjs` itself flips**, producing a deterministic false "identical" precisely when a restart was coming. The reviewer also corrected my framing: a false "identical" never skips a write (`saveConfig` is unconditional) — it skips the **health poll**, i.e. hands control back mid-restart, which is the original outage's exact mechanism. Fixes applied: fingerprint rewritten against the real predicate with **no sorting** (CCR's compare is order-sensitive and it performs no normalizing sort, so canonicalizing hid real changes); **`waitForGateway` made unconditional** — the structural fix that converts any future mispredict into one extra HTTP request; a failed health poll is now **fatal** rather than a warning, so it can no longer end a run with the restore points deleted; `acquireLock` rewritten around `fs.openSync(…, "wx")` because `existsSync`-then-write is not a mutex (verified with two concurrent runs: one completes, one refuses); `isProcessAlive` now treats **`EPERM` as alive**, since an elevated owner seen from a non-elevated run would otherwise be judged dead and its live lock stolen; lock release only removes a lock we own, and signal handlers stop Ctrl+C leaking it; `retainOnSuccess` moved **out of the try**, where a cleanup failure was rolling back an already-verified-good write and advertising a snapshot it had just deleted; `atomicWriteJson` was written but **never called** (the real write was still non-atomic) — now wired, with destination-ACL preservation, since a rename silently replaces an explicit DACL with an inherited one and *widens* access, plus temp cleanup and a sharing-violation retry; `restoreSettings` made atomic too, being the one writer that runs when things are already wrong; single-quote escaping added for the PowerShell and **SQL** literals (a username containing an apostrophe is legal and would break the `VACUUM INTO` on the "refusing to write without a restore point" path); `otherClaudeSessions` now excludes our own ancestor sessions (the previous `n !== process.pid` filter was a no-op, so running keysync from inside a session reported that session as one it was about to interrupt); and `--no-profile` no longer mutates the profile at all — it had been persisting `settingsFile: <real settings.json>` and the model tiers into CCR's DB, which any later CCR-initiated apply would have acted on, a deferred replay of the outage — while the settings backup is now taken unconditionally and the WIF-token cleanup runs in that mode too.
- v2, round 8 (2026-09-02): **Phase 4 implemented** (single-writer lock, content-diff-and-skip, gateway health poll, other-session warning, atomic settings write), verified in isolation with live state untouched. Two bugs in the diff logic were found by testing rather than review, both of which would have made the skip never fire: comparing a freshly built payload against CCR's *persisted* config always differs because CCR enriches persisted objects, and CCR **omits `enabled: true`** when persisting since it is the default, so comparing key presence reported a change every run. Both fixed by projecting each side onto the fields keysync owns and normalizing defaults to their meaning. A third, smaller one: the lock originally surfaced as a raw stack trace. Separately, a user question about per-provider model counts exposed a **verification ratchet defect** — `verify-cli.mjs` probes only rows already in the picker, so a row pruned for a transient failure (`mistral`, a 503) can never return. Recorded as Phase 6 item 4 along with the live `/v1/models` discovery option, which the user deferred for a later discussion; that item also documents that "Cut from v2" item 2's rationale has been invalidated by measurement. The deferred `SessionStart` hook remains out of scope, and its restart-suppression precondition is void with the watchdog gone.
- v2, round 7 (2026-09-02): **Phase 1 deprecated rather than fixed, and replaced by OS-level process supervision.** Prompted by the user asking the right question — if we shipped without the watchdog, is it needed at all? The answer separated the *need* (real, and larger post-ship: the CCR gateway and the OAuth relay are both now in the critical path, which they were not before UW) from the *artifact* (wrong shape: its flakiness premise had evaporated after Phase 0.5's root-cause fix, and three review rounds each found a regression introduced by the previous fix). Deprecating it also **eliminated** rather than mitigated the watchdog-versus-keysync restart-collision risk, which in turn **unblocked Phases 4 and 5** — that dependency existed only because a healer existed. Replacement is stateless: a scheduled task (at logon + every 5 min) running an ensure-running check that starts the relay if 4517 is not listening and `ccr start` if 3456 is not listening; it cannot fight anything, since it only starts what is not already up. Runs as the logged-on user, non-elevated, by necessity — both processes depend on DPAPI-scoped user state. Verified by killing the live relay and confirming the task restored it, that the replacement was no longer parented to an interactive session, and that Claude still routed. This also fixed a latent live defect found while answering the question: the relay serving 4517 was a child of an agent session and would have died with it, and the earlier manual attempt to re-parent it had silently failed because `Get-Process` does not expose `CommandLine`. All downstream references (Risks, Phase 5's kill-recovery step, Phase 6's `SessionStart` gate, the status header, the runbook) were updated; the original Phase 1 text is retained but explicitly marked VOID.
- v2, round 6 (2026-09-02, document revision — no new execution): brought the body into line with what was actually built, because the document had become a stale plan wrapped in append-only corrections, and a reader following the body would have been misled on several safety-relevant points. Corrected **in place**, each marked rather than silently rewritten: the Status header (now states what shipped, what is running live, and the four things explicitly *not* done); "keysync is a Rust binary" → Node, with the deviation flagged at the top of Phase 3; the catalog-before-`testModel` ordering, which measurement had reversed; the `modelPicker` schema, which was missing `behavesAs`; the "verified so far" scoping in v2 Architecture, which understated routing (now proven) while overstating the picker (still unproven); the `Providers[] == 44` criterion and the `cargo-audit` gate, both superseded; the `replaceBuiltInOptions` rationale, whose unchallenged premise ("zero Anthropic entries") is what produced Finding 9; the Phase 1 heading, which claimed to be a blocking dependency for work that then shipped past it; and the two-hand-made-patches follow-up, whose prediction came true. **Most consequentially, the desktop-app established fact was inverted**: it asserted the sync is gated on `surface !== "cli"`, which is true of the TypeScript source reference but *false in the shipped dist*, where the only gate is "a provider model exists". Acting on that wrong fact is what rewrote the real desktop app's config. A companion fact was added: **the shipped dist diverges from the source reference this document cites throughout, so verify against `dist/main/cli.js`, not `ccr-src-reference`.** A "How to run it" runbook was added. The changelog itself is left append-only as the authoritative history.
- v2, round 4 → round 5 (same session, 2026-09-01, after building the isolated harness): Phase 2.5's **routing half passed** with three-way attribution, and the **CLI path passed**; the interactive `/model` picker remains unexercised and Phase 2.5 is therefore not fully closed. The run produced four findings, three of which contradict assumptions this document made: (1) the chosen attribution marker `thought_signature` is **stripped by CCR's response translation** — CCR's own request log is the mechanism that works; (2) CCR **ignores an explicit provider protocol**, overriding both `type` and `protocolDetectionMode: "manual"` with host-inferred `gemini_generate_content` — a direct threat to Phase 3's plan to write 44 providers with vault-derived protocols; (3) CCR's `applyProfile` appends **`[1m]`** to `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` for 1M-capable models and Claude Code rejects the suffixed id, **breaking small/fast background traffic** — empirical confirmation of the round-3 hazard that had only been reasoned about; (4) `modelPicker` rows support **`behavesAs`** (and a separate `modelOverrides`) to map unrecognized provider-format ids, without which an unknown model is assumed to have a **200k context window** — which would silently apply to the 23-of-44 providers on a single unknown `testModel`, and bears on Phase 5's context-window test. Harness review before the run was substantive: the critic returned REJECT (the spike as first written made no request at all yet printed a success line, and checked `Router` state after the mutation by printing rather than asserting), and the security review returned HIGH with the counterintuitive finding that `profile.enabled = false` *arms* the live-settings write path. Both were fixed before the key was used; the isolation contract gained `LOCALAPPDATA` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` levers and lost `surface: "cli"` as a safety control. Live state was verified unchanged throughout, and the scratch tree (including the provider key, the `apiKeyHelper` `.cmd`, and the WIF token file) was deleted at teardown.
- v2, round 3 → round 4 (new session, 2026-09-01, execution attempt rather than review): the first attempt at Phase 2.5 caused a **live self-inflicted outage** — `saveConfig({applyProfile: true})` rewrote the global `~/.claude/settings.json` that a running Claude Code session depended on, then the gateway restart failed to rebind, leaving that session pointed at a dead gateway with an unsupported pinned model; a separate agent had to disable CCR, restore `settings.json` from backup, and neutralize `global-profile-takeover.json`. Three findings recorded from it, all new and none caught by three rounds of review: (1) **the outage mechanism is a design hazard, not just a test error** — Phase 4's manual refresh is specified to be invoked from inside a Claude Code session, and would do the same thing to that session; Phase 4 must address it explicitly. (2) **CCR auto-reconfigures the real Anthropic Claude desktop app** whenever `Providers[]` is non-empty and a `claude-code` profile has `surface !== "cli"`, which is the default — added as an established fact and a keysync requirement (`surface: "cli"` on every owned profile); this also explains the two files initially found unexplained on this machine. (3) **Two config stores exist** (MSIX-virtualized vs plain), only the plain one is the correct target, and writes to the wrong one silently appear to succeed. A mandatory four-lever test-isolation contract (separate `CCR_INTERNAL_APP_DATA_DIR`, separate ports, scratch `profile.settingsFile`, `claude --settings`) is now a blocking precondition on Phase 2.5 and every later live test. Verified-good side effects of the attempt, kept: the vault filter really does yield 46 registry keys → 44 distinct providers exactly as the plan states, `google` reduces to a single key (`personal.google.free`) after filtering, that key is live (HTTP 200, `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`), and Google's responses carry a provider-distinctive `extra_content.google.thought_signature` field that satisfies Phase 2.5's attribution requirement. Phase 2.5 itself remains **not run**.
- v2, round 2 → round 3 (same session, all three reviewers re-run against the round-2 fix-pass): critic confirmed the CRITICAL finding genuinely closed and most prior findings resolved, but found the fix pass itself had reproduced the same overclaim pattern C1 was about — two fresh "verified live" claims attached to the `modelPicker`/routing mechanism, which Phase 2(d) never actually tested (only credential precedence was tested; routing rests on documentation text pending Phase 2.5) — corrected throughout, most consequentially in the `v2 Architecture` bullet and the ADR's "Why chosen." Critic also found a real arithmetic error in the round-2 multi-key tie-break list (miscounted which providers survive the vault filter — corrected from 5 to the actual 2, `groq` and `deepseek`, with the tie-break's actual arbitrary 19-second outcome now named explicitly rather than hidden behind a rule that doesn't discriminate); a `replaceBuiltInOptions: false` decision whose stated rationale was backwards (CCR clears all model-alias env vars every run regardless — reversed to `true`); a newly-surfaced coverage gap (the pricing catalog covers only 21 of 44 filtered providers, not "most"); a Phase 5 test case that tested the safest possible shape instead of the actually dangerous one (corrected from a namespaced id to a bare one); and that Phase 2.5, the earliest and most consequential gate, had no check against being false-greened by CCR's own fallback chain (added). Architect independently found the same 44-provider arithmetic error, plus that `settings.json`'s own background/small-fast-tier model env vars are cleared and never repopulated by any draft so far (added as an explicit Phase 3 requirement and Phase 5 test), and that the write-ordering fix only covered keysync's own two writers, not an externally-triggered restart racing them (Phase 1/2.5/3 restart-collision note extended). Security-reviewer re-confirmed H1/L1/L2/L3 as resolved but found three of the round-2 fix's own new requirements were specified in a way that would be implemented wrong by default: the credential-redaction newtype's `Serialize` behavior was unspecified (could silently write `"[redacted]"` into live config instead of the real key — now specified explicitly, with a test), the `config.sqlite` backup method didn't account for CCR's WAL-mode storage (a plain file copy would silently produce an invalid restore point — corrected to a WAL-safe snapshot method, plus DPAPI-encrypting the backup and switching retention from count-only to delete-on-success), and the WIF-file cleanup was one-shot when CCR recreates the file on every profile apply from any source (corrected to a recurring, pinned-filename deletion, with the actually-correct reason — CCR's own cleanup targets the other file, not this one). Also found a new alias-collision hazard specific to the 44-provider scale (added as an explicit Phase 3 assertion) and that `cargo-audit` isn't installed on this machine at all (gate corrected from "not yet run" to a real setup step). Not yet re-reviewed after this second fold-in — given three rounds of substantive, independently-verified findings with diminishing severity each round (round 3's issues are corrections to round 2's own text, not new categories of gap), further rounds are expected to hit diminishing returns; the next natural gate is Phase 2.5 itself, which will produce live evidence rather than another documentation review.
